 his mother they set off on  a journey that takes them all the way to  Italy Mike finds himself falling for  Scots and the two have to navigate the  intense emotions tied up in their  complicated relationship as a result the  film is very different from Reeves's  more action-heavy movies but it proved  to a skeptical world that the actor  really did have some serious dramatic  chops replicas was universally panned by  critics and it wasn't much of a hit with  audiences either to be fair to Reeves  though he was probably the only bright  spot in this practically unwatchable  film but tries he might his performance  just couldn't Salvage This truly dire  movie Reeves plays William Foster a  neuroscientist who Ventures into some  pretty unethical territory while caught  in the throes of grief after his wife  and children die in a car crash she  decides to try cloning them he knows he  has to keep this experiment to secret  but he can't hide it forever and  naturally this risky decision comes with  some unintended consequences admittedly  the idea behind replicas is actually  quite interesting and with better  execution this could have been a far  more engaging film but with numerous  plot holes and a cast of characters who  just can't seem to stop themselves from  making the worst possible decision in  every scenario watching replicas becomes  little more than an exercise in  frustration  speed was one of Reeves's first major  Blockbuster films and was arguably the  movie that catapulted him to Superstar  status Reeve stars as Jack traven a SWAT  officer working with the LAPD Jack and  his partner think they've managed to  stop extortionist bomber Howard Payne  after he attempted to hold a group of  elevator passengers for ransom as it  turns out however pain is also rigged a  bus with a bomb and if the vehicle's  speed drops below 50 miles per hour it  will explode and kill everyone on board  now it's up to Jack to defuse the  explosive and get everyone to safety  admittedly the premise of speed comes  across as kind of silly but as the movie  picks up it's nearly impossible to look  away from the screen the genuinely  sizzling on-screen Chemistry Between  Reeves and his co-star Sandra Bullock  certainly didn't hurt either and then of  course there's the bus jump one of  Hollywood's best ever driving stunts and  it was totally real too  foreign  all in all this is Reeves at his 90s  best just don't mention the sequel  based on a William Gibson story of the  same name Johnny Mnemonic sees Reef Star  as its titular character the plot is  clever enough Johnny has a cybernetic  brain implant that can store all kinds  of information and in a dystopian  Society ruled by Mega corporations where  everyone is forced to be connected to  the internet his abilities come in very  handy indeed in Johnny's World  transmitting sensitive data can be  dangerous so as a mnemonic Courier it's  up to him to carry certain information  to different parties however doing so  means risking losing his childhood  memories and he wants to remove his  implant to get them back unfortunately  for Johnny however his work isn't quite  done yet Johnny Mnemonic didn't exactly  get a glowing response from the critics  in fact Reeves actually earned himself a  Razzie nomination for worst actor so  it's safe to say that this role was a  new low point in his career although it  was marketed as an exciting cyberpunk  Thriller the plot Falls totally flat and  worse still it is aged very badly indeed  even for a 90s film sadly there's just  nothing all that memorable about the  futuristic world of Johnny Mnemonic The  Matrix was more than just the film it  was a cultural phenomenon the movie won  four Academy Awards and left a lasting  impact on an entire generation of  filmmakers and while Reeves was already  a well-known actor when the Matrix was  released this film brought his career to  a whole new level in this masterpiece  from the Wachowski sisters Reeve stars  is Neo a hacker who's unknowingly living  in a dystopia where Humanity has been  trapped in a simulated reality called  The Matrix this simulation was designed  by machines so they could control human  beings by keeping them in pots all while  harvesting their bodies for energy when  Neo discovers the truth about his world  he's given a choice he can take the blue  pill and return to his old life or he  can take the red pill and finally accept  reality and even though he knows he can  never truly go back Neo nonetheless  steps up to lead the Rebellion Against  the Machines the resulting movie is full  to the brim with philosophical musings  incredible martial arts fights and some  of the most recognizable and iconic  quotes in cinematic history  I know Kung Fu  show me The Matrix also gave audiences a  truly classic villain and agent Smith  gifted film fans with one of the coolest  opening scenes ever plus one of Cinema's  craziest gunfights the movie  revolutionized Hollywood with its  innovative camera work and brought  Newfound Fame and respect to Reeves  himself who delivered a fascinating  zen-like performance honestly The Matrix  isn't just one of Keanu Reeves's  greatest films it's one of the greatest  science fiction films of all time  Generation Um however might just be the  worst out of Reeves's entire career even  the title is underwhelming and things  don't exactly improve from there this  Mumble core Indie flake was so  unwatchable that it actually managed to  score a whopping zero percent on Rotten  Tomatoes in Generation Um Reeves plays  Jon an escort service driver who's lost  all sense of direction in life the film  explores 24 hours of his aimless  existence in New York City in which he  meets up with two young women Violet and  Mia this is an attempt to delve into the  concept of urban alienation but the  movie Falls far short of its attention  in one scene for example Jon points a  camcorder at the women and asks them to  say something interesting